        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Oceania</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Lapita culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The western Pacific, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bismarck-archipelago">Bismarck Archipelago</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a> through the Reef and Santa Cruz Islands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a> and New Caledonia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, Tonga and Samoa</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1500–500 BCE: in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bismarck-archipelago">Bismarck Archipelago</a> from c. 1500–1300 BCE, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a>, New Caledonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> by around 1000 BCE, in Tonga c. 880 BCE and Samoa c. 800 BCE; its decorated pottery gone by c. 500 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">Dentate-stamped pottery with geometric designs and stylised faces; open-sea voyages that settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a>, New Caledonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>; obsidian carried thousands of kilometres; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teouma">Teouma</a> cemetery; its place as the ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavated sites such as Lapita in New Caledonia, Talepakemalai in the Mussau Islands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teouma">Teouma</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a>, Nukuleka in Tonga and Mulifanua in Samoa; studies of pottery and obsidian; radiocarbon and uranium–thorium dates; ancient DNA; the reconstructed Proto-Oceanic language; no writing</span></div>
