    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Lapita culture, named after a site in New Caledonia and known by its finely stamped pottery, appeared in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bismarck-archipelago">Bismarck Archipelago</a> north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> between about 1500 and 1300 BCE.</li><li>Within a few centuries its seafarers crossed hundreds of kilometres of open ocean to become the first people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a>, New Caledonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, Tonga and Samoa.</li><li>Descended largely from Austronesian voyagers of Island <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, they probably spoke the ancestor of the Oceanic languages, and their descendants in Tonga and Samoa became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The name Lapita comes from a misunderstanding: at the New Caledonian site dug in 1952, archaeologists took it from xapeta’a, a word in the Haveke language meaning “to dig a hole” or “the place where one digs”.</p></aside>
    </section>

